WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.297
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:09.330
CLEMENS OERTEL:
Welcome, everyone.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.430
Thank you for joining us today.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:14.090
What we would like to
talk to you about today

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:17.150
is the initial steps of
our journey at the Imagia

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:20.540
to build a collaboration
ecosystem to help bring AI

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:22.130
to health care.

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:24.800
And right off the bat,
please allow me to say this

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.632
is my very first talk in public.

00:00:26.632 --> 00:00:28.340
So if I seem nervous,
it is because I am.

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:29.180
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.010
Thank you.

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:35.960
May we do a quick initial survey
just to get a feeling for you

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:37.580
in the audience?

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:40.220
May I ask those in the audience
who work in health care

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.420
to quickly raise their hand?

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.780
Yeah, that's a good number.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:50.360
And who of those are actually
involved in clinical research?

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:52.400
That's less.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:58.250
And who is already deploying
AI systems in production?

00:00:58.250 --> 00:00:58.750
Smaller.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:02.140
And last question
in that direction,

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:06.780
who is using AI in
clinical research?

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.190
Congratulations.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:14.670
And if we may ask one personal
question, who in the audience

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:19.312
knows someone who has
been affected by cancer?

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:25.000
That's a very large number.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.410
So without further ado,
my name is Clemens Oertel

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:33.200
or, for the ones who know German
pronunciation, Clemens Oertel.

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:38.590
I have been working as a
program manager and software

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:42.760
architect at Imagia,
helping in its federations

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:44.132
and traceability efforts.

00:01:44.132 --> 00:01:45.590
JONATHAN KANEVSKY:
Thanks, Clement.

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:46.960
CLEMENS OERTEL: Jonathan.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:48.335
JONATHAN KANEVSKY:
Hi, everybody.

00:01:48.335 --> 00:01:50.170
My name is Jonathan
Kanevsky, and I'm

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:53.050
a board-certified reconstructive
surgeon based here

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:54.140
in California.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:56.440
And I've been leading
the team at Imagia

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:59.460
in the area of
clinical innovation.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:06.676
So before we jump into our talk
on data, AI, and health care,

00:02:06.676 --> 00:02:08.259
Clemens and I wanted
to share with you

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:10.990
some words from a
health care visionary

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:12.850
who really foreshadowed
the explosion

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:16.170
of big data in health.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:18.540
Dr. Larry Weed, who
you see up there,

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.270
he coined the term
knowledge couplers.

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:24.270
He gave a famous grand rounds
lecture in the Massachusetts

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.600
General Hospital in
1960, and he believed

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.940
that there was no way that
doctors could possibly

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:32.860
manage all the data that
was related to a patient.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:35.280
He's responsible for creating
today's concept of what

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:36.822
is a medical record.

00:02:36.822 --> 00:02:38.280
And I just wanted
to share with you

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.580
what he had to say over 60 years
ago about big data and health

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:42.080
care.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:50.662
LARRY WEED: What I'd like
to do is go at this problem

00:02:50.662 --> 00:02:52.370
not from the point of
view of the record.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:54.370
I mean, we really aren't
taking care of records.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.150
We are taking care of people.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:03:00.050
And we're trying to get across
the idea that this record

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:03.830
cannot be separated from
the caring of that patient.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:08.870
This is not something the
practice of medicine over here

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:10.610
and the record over here.

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:12.355
This is the practice
of medicine.

00:03:12.355 --> 00:03:14.240
It's intertwined with it.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:17.360
It determines what you
do in the long run.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:21.770
You're a victim of it or
you're a triumph because of it.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:26.480
The human mind simply cannot
carry all the information about

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.740
all the patients in a
practice without error.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:32.675
And so the--

00:03:32.675 --> 00:03:34.050
JONATHAN KANEVSKY:
So you can see

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:36.330
that this idea that
big data in health

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:39.000
is really not
something that's novel.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.370
But here we are today sitting
almost six years later

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:44.370
discussing it, and I think Dr.
Larry Weed would be completely

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:47.880
ecstatic to know that we're
making so much effort in health

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:48.380
care and AI.

00:03:56.329 --> 00:03:58.370
So I'd like to tell you
a bit about my experience

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:00.710
and why I'm interested
in health AI

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:03.650
before I give you the background
of our company, Imagia.

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:05.780
My interest in the I
started during residency

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:07.460
where, as a surgical
resident, I was

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:12.620
exposed to a number of patients
who were really suffering

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:13.880
in the medical system--

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:16.610
in particular, one
patient, a young girl

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:18.769
who died of metastatic
breast cancer, who

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:21.170
had a tumor that was
completely missed

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:23.180
by a screening mammogram.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:25.100
And I remember shortly
after she passed away,

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:28.100
I spent the evening poring
through her mammograms trying

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.484
to find a tumor that
just wasn't there,

00:04:30.484 --> 00:04:32.150
thinking that there's
got to be some way

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:34.040
that the human eye
just isn't capturing

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:37.430
what's clinically there.

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:40.730
So I was lucky enough to meet
a team of computer scientists

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:43.700
at Imagia who were
working on these problems

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:45.350
and wanted to team
up and try and find

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:47.720
a way to bring AI solutions
that would actually

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.970
transform health care.

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:52.420
So Imagia was founded in 2015.

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:54.680
We're a company of
more than 40 employees,

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:58.050
and we have close ties to
the Montreal AI ecosystem.

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:00.440
We have a great relationship
with the Montreal Institute

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:04.550
for Learning Algorithms, one of
the largest groups of postdocs

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:07.185
doing research in AI in Canada.

00:05:07.185 --> 00:05:09.560
And we have active collaboration
with over five hospitals

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:12.580
in North America and Europe.

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:15.520
So today, Clemens and I want to
share with you our big picture

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:16.757
mission.

00:05:16.757 --> 00:05:19.090
We also want to share with
you some technical details as

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:22.300
to how we are building an
ecosystem that powers discovery

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:24.770
for clinical applications.

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:27.645
So how do we impact a
patient's journey with AI?

00:05:27.645 --> 00:05:32.260
A good example that we are
focusing on is lung cancer.

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:35.810
From screening with
X-ray or low-dose CT

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:38.390
to later in the patient's
journey with diagnosis

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:43.410
and biopsy, then to treatment
with targeted chemotherapies,

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:47.310
and finally with surgery and
then the long road to recovery,

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:50.610
each step of the patient's
journey is important to us.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:53.490
Creating an AI tool for
each step of the journey

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:56.130
leads to a
comprehensive care that

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:59.670
focuses on value-based outcomes
such as quality of life,

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:03.429
not just mortality.

00:06:03.429 --> 00:06:04.970
The first step of
a patient's journey

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:07.130
in the long road of what
it means to be diagnosed

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:11.570
with lung cancer is screening.

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:14.690
Lung cancer is the leading
cause of cancer-related death.

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:18.150
Despite the best efforts made to
screen the general population--

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:23.230
mostly smokers-- many patients
with lung cancer do not smoke.

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:25.450
Where we want to
deliver the value of AI

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:28.240
is detecting patients
early, stage one disease,

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:31.234
where it's most easily treated.

00:06:31.234 --> 00:06:32.650
Screening is usually
the beginning

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:36.250
of a patient journey
that is filled with fear,

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:38.710
many unknowns, and often
made worse by the fact

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:42.640
that many patients are diagnosed
with advanced-stage disease.

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:44.740
Being diagnosed with
late-stage disease

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:49.300
means you have almost 20 times
more likelihood of death.

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:51.040
So finding the disease
at an early stage

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:52.540
is the best way to
improve outcomes.

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:57.790
Once that lung
cancer is detected,

00:06:57.790 --> 00:07:00.220
the process of
establishing a diagnosis

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:04.000
is often a long,
cumbersome process

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:07.560
followed by choosing
the right treatment.

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:12.300
So why is this process so
time-consuming and confusing?

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:14.160
Well, it's difficult to know.

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:17.670
Should all patients be given the
most expensive molecular tests

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:19.080
right away?

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:21.690
Should a patient be started
on first-line therapy

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:24.214
if they have
advanced-stage disease,

00:07:24.214 --> 00:07:26.630
or should they be advanced to
targeted immunotherapy right

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:30.580
from the time of diagnosis?

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:33.060
So in today's paradigm,
there's really two ways

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:34.800
to establish a diagnosis.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:38.280
It's either tissue biopsy, which
is the current gold standard,

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.260
or a liquid biopsy.

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:46.070
Tissue biopsies are when
a surgeon or an oncologist

00:07:46.070 --> 00:07:48.700
will take a piece of tissue
and send to a pathologist.

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:52.580
It's often not as good
of a gold standard

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:55.000
as we would imagine because,
up to 40% of the time,

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:58.160
a repeat biopsy is needed
or the tissue that was taken

00:07:58.160 --> 00:07:59.990
wasn't sent for the
right test to establish

00:07:59.990 --> 00:08:00.860
the right diagnosis.

00:08:03.830 --> 00:08:07.160
Up and coming is a technique
called liquid biopsy.

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:10.550
It's growing in popularity,
and what it entails

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:13.880
is taking and sampling
blood from a patient

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:16.670
and looking for bits of
tumor DNA that has shed--

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:18.357
much like a dog sheds hair.

00:08:18.357 --> 00:08:20.690
But, as you can imagine, that
also has its own problems.

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:23.120
Just like there are dogs that
shed a lot of hair and dogs

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:28.240
that shed very little hair, not
all tumors shed DNA equally.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:30.850
Up to 25% of these
cancers don't shed

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:35.360
DNA, which means a
liquid biopsy would fail.

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:37.570
So what's happening is
that, in many hospitals

00:08:37.570 --> 00:08:41.620
and many clinical settings,
specialists-- pathologists,

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:44.190
oncologists, surgeons--
despite their best efforts

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:47.770
to communicate, they're
functioning in silos.

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:50.420
They're not interacting
as closely as they should.

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:52.840
And if we want to be successful
in treating patients,

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:55.300
we need to transcend
the silos and use

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:56.940
AI to accelerate diagnosis.

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:02.670
After establishing a
diagnosis, the next step

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:06.470
in a patient's journey
is optimizing treatment.

00:09:06.470 --> 00:09:10.460
Precision medicine initiated
a revolution 15 years ago.

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:13.100
However, it's yet to create the
transformational change that

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:15.590
impacts patients
from day to day.

00:09:15.590 --> 00:09:17.180
For example, there
are very few drugs

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:19.454
that target known gene
mutations for cancer,

00:09:19.454 --> 00:09:20.870
and we still don't
have a good way

00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:26.010
of selecting patients for
treatment for immunotherapy.

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:28.350
The majority of patients that
are diagnosed with cancer

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:31.279
will not have the option to
be treated with immunotherapy.

00:09:31.279 --> 00:09:32.820
This is because
patients usually need

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:36.060
to fail a first or a second-line
treatment that is not

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:39.210
tumor specific before
their tumor is rebiopsied

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:44.821
to see if it can be targeted
by a specific immunotherapy.

00:09:44.821 --> 00:09:47.070
Even when patients are put
on the right immunotherapy,

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:49.500
as much as 90% of those
patients will have tumors

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:53.781
that are resistant to the drug.

00:09:53.781 --> 00:09:56.030
And that's because their
small cohorts of cancer cells

00:09:56.030 --> 00:10:00.434
within that population
that are just resistant.

00:10:00.434 --> 00:10:02.100
So with our platform,
what we hope to do

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:05.340
is empower physicians to
use AI to identify features

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:08.100
invisible to the eye that
could help guide patients

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:11.040
to the appropriate therapy and
avoid dangerous and expensive

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:12.510
biopsies and molecular tests.

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:17.240
One example of
where this plays is

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:20.520
in the area of deep radiomics.

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:22.180
So radiomics is
an evolving field

00:10:22.180 --> 00:10:24.685
where we believe that there
is information captured

00:10:24.685 --> 00:10:28.990
in an image that is just not
accessible to the human eye--

00:10:28.990 --> 00:10:33.410
patterns that relate to
disease, for example.

00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:35.590
Is there a specific
morphology to a tumor

00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:37.540
that could indicate
its genetic status?

00:10:37.540 --> 00:10:40.210
Could a lung cancer be
EGFR-positive just based

00:10:40.210 --> 00:10:41.895
on its radiomic fingerprint?

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:47.820
Where this comes into
play is an immunotherapy.

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:51.225
So by show of hands,
who had lunch today?

00:10:51.225 --> 00:10:53.600
Most people here, but some
people may have skipped lunch.

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:55.530
Some people may have
skipped breakfast.

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:57.530
But if I asked everybody
here to run a marathon,

00:10:57.530 --> 00:10:59.660
everybody would
perform differently--

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:03.620
the same way if I was to give
an immunotherapy to everybody

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:06.710
who was able to take
that immunotherapy.

00:11:06.710 --> 00:11:08.870
Their bodies would
react differently

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:11.930
because everybody's immune
system has a different makeup.

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:15.020
And in immunotherapy for cancer,
you need your immune system

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:18.440
to be primed appropriately to
combine with that immunotherapy

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:20.489
to target the cancer.

00:11:20.489 --> 00:11:22.280
So this is why many
immunotherapy therapies

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:26.090
fail or have such a
varied range of results--

00:11:26.090 --> 00:11:29.390
because we don't know the state
of somebody's immune system

00:11:29.390 --> 00:11:33.650
as well as we should before
that immunotherapy is given.

00:11:33.650 --> 00:11:36.350
This is where deep
radiomics can have an effect

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:39.350
and give insights where we
didn't have them before.

00:11:39.350 --> 00:11:44.270
Can we train our AI
models on CTs or MRIs

00:11:44.270 --> 00:11:46.100
combined with
pathology information

00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:48.980
from immunohistochemistry
to identify

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:52.230
patient's immune status before
we give them immunotherapy

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:54.936
so we know when is the ideal
time to give an immunotherapy

00:11:54.936 --> 00:11:56.060
to achieve the best result?

00:11:59.030 --> 00:12:01.470
So finally, patients
have been screened.

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:02.754
They've been diagnosed.

00:12:02.754 --> 00:12:04.170
Their treatment
has been delivered

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:06.180
in an optimal fashion.

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:09.390
But now begins a long
road to recovery.

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:12.570
Chemotherapy can leave
patients with heart problems,

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:15.840
gastrointestinal
issues, skin damage.

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:17.880
Radiotherapy causes
irreversible damage

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:20.860
to the skin and
the immune system.

00:12:20.860 --> 00:12:23.310
If you think about it, we're
really living in an age

00:12:23.310 --> 00:12:25.110
where people are
surviving their cancer,

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:28.230
but now suffering more from
the treatment of the cancer

00:12:28.230 --> 00:12:30.930
than the disease itself.

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:33.960
Treating cancer is a noble
and monumental achievement,

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:37.410
but now we must also focus on
minimizing the morbidity that

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:40.140
comes with cancer.

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:43.240
To transform this
delivery of care,

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:45.280
we have to treat
beyond a given disease

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:47.350
and focus on developing
treatment strategies that

00:12:47.350 --> 00:12:49.450
can minimize harm
to the patient based

00:12:49.450 --> 00:12:50.870
on their unique
biological makeup.

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:56.900
As we build our
discovery platform

00:12:56.900 --> 00:12:59.990
to power clinical adoption,
it's important to remember

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:03.170
just how imperfect the
medical training system is.

00:13:03.170 --> 00:13:05.090
Information is
constantly evolving

00:13:05.090 --> 00:13:07.800
in academic silos
around the world,

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:09.860
and the main medium for
sharing this information

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:12.860
is through publications
and journals.

00:13:12.860 --> 00:13:14.510
As a doctor, I can
tell you it's nearly

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:18.320
impossible for a physician today
to remain accurately up to date

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:20.960
on the latest advances
in medical science,

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:23.770
and it's even more difficult to
actually employ these advances

00:13:23.770 --> 00:13:26.035
into medical practice.

00:13:26.035 --> 00:13:27.410
So building a
platform that makes

00:13:27.410 --> 00:13:31.130
use of federated medical
data across a network

00:13:31.130 --> 00:13:33.320
will bring us one step
closer to understanding some

00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:34.528
of the randomness we observe.

00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:39.910
So before we shift gears from
the medical to technical side

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:42.940
of the talk, I just
want to leave you

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:46.120
with this point, which is what
we're trying to build at Imagia

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:48.910
is an ecosystem where we
empower doctors to bring

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:52.540
their clinical expertise to
create clinical adoption,

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:55.750
and we bring AI expertise
to bring about discovery,

00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:59.650
and ultimately together to make
AI that transforms health care.

00:13:59.650 --> 00:14:02.440
So I'm going to pass the
mic over to Clemens now

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:04.670
to share with you a bit
of how this magic happens

00:14:04.670 --> 00:14:06.436
and the technical details.

00:14:06.436 --> 00:14:08.710
CLEMENS OERTEL: Thank
you very much, Jonathan.

00:14:08.710 --> 00:14:12.250
So based on what
Jonathan just showed us,

00:14:12.250 --> 00:14:15.490
we at Imagia believe
that AI tasks related

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:17.830
to clinical questions
are best formulated

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:20.110
by clinicians themselves.

00:14:20.110 --> 00:14:24.040
However, most clinicians
are not AI experts,

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:25.630
and that is exactly
what we're trying

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:28.600
to enable the clinicians
to do with the platform

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:31.630
that we're currently building
as Imagia based on Google Cloud

00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:32.980
Platform--

00:14:32.980 --> 00:14:36.790
namely, to use AI in the
clinicians' discoveries

00:14:36.790 --> 00:14:41.090
without having to know
about the details of AI.

00:14:41.090 --> 00:14:44.170
Jonathan already mentioned a
few of the possible questions

00:14:44.170 --> 00:14:45.790
in the clinical
field where AI might

00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:48.490
come in handy, such
as, could be predict

00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:50.770
the efficacy of a
certain treatment for one

00:14:50.770 --> 00:14:51.960
particular patient--

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:55.870
not for a big statistical
base, but for one patient--

00:14:55.870 --> 00:14:58.560
based on information that
has been acquired early

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:00.510
in the continuum of care?

00:15:00.510 --> 00:15:03.850
Today, most of the time, we
have standard treatment plans.

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:07.780
We apply the most commonly
applicable treatment first,

00:15:07.780 --> 00:15:09.830
and only if that
doesn't work do we go on

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:13.690
to the more advanced,
more costly treatments.

00:15:13.690 --> 00:15:17.350
And this might make sense
from a cost perspective,

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:19.160
from a public
health perspective.

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:20.740
But for an individual
patient, this

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:23.810
might not be the best approach.

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:26.630
Also, could be
determined the malignancy

00:15:26.630 --> 00:15:29.510
of a tumor just based
on early imaging,

00:15:29.510 --> 00:15:32.300
such as the EGFR
status of a tumor?

00:15:32.300 --> 00:15:34.700
This way, a clinician could
choose the right treatment

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:36.500
early on.

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:38.540
Or, to express it
more generally,

00:15:38.540 --> 00:15:43.040
can we predict patient outcome
based on raw data acquired

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:45.820
early in the continuum of care?

00:15:45.820 --> 00:15:47.830
To answer these
questions, one usually

00:15:47.830 --> 00:15:50.880
conducts a retrospective
clinical study.

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:54.430
And to give you a little
bit of a background,

00:15:54.430 --> 00:15:56.200
to conduct such a
study the first step

00:15:56.200 --> 00:15:59.000
is normally to build a
patient cohort for that study

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:02.300
and for that outcome.

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:05.200
To build a patient cohort means
to find suitable patients based

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:10.060
on past diagnostic or treatment
results, such as a drug worked

00:16:10.060 --> 00:16:14.740
or did not work, tumor
was malignant or benign.

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:17.050
And that usually involves
going through vast amounts

00:16:17.050 --> 00:16:18.730
of clinical records--
textual records.

00:16:18.730 --> 00:16:20.501
This is actually
clinicians sitting

00:16:20.501 --> 00:16:22.000
there sifting through
these records,

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:24.880
finding the right records,
the right patients

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:27.580
because these records
are not structured

00:16:27.580 --> 00:16:29.650
for that kind of questioning.

00:16:29.650 --> 00:16:32.090
And that's a tedious,
laborsome task.

00:16:32.090 --> 00:16:34.990
And one of the
toolkit or tooling

00:16:34.990 --> 00:16:38.620
that we had Imagia
developed to assist in this

00:16:38.620 --> 00:16:40.900
is, based on NLP
and active learning,

00:16:40.900 --> 00:16:44.130
a clinician can just
tag a few reports.

00:16:44.130 --> 00:16:46.090
We then can go through
all the records

00:16:46.090 --> 00:16:49.630
that there are, find
the best matches

00:16:49.630 --> 00:16:52.600
based on these early
selections, and this

00:16:52.600 --> 00:16:56.170
may build up a larger cohort.

00:16:56.170 --> 00:16:57.910
And of course,
unfortunately, this

00:16:57.910 --> 00:17:00.640
has to be happening
on premise because we

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:04.849
do need access to the full
data corpus at the hospital.

00:17:04.849 --> 00:17:06.695
The next step,
just as difficult,

00:17:06.695 --> 00:17:09.510
is to find the corresponding
imaging data because remember,

00:17:09.510 --> 00:17:12.890
we want to be able to, based
on very early imaging that

00:17:12.890 --> 00:17:15.319
was acquired before
we even knew what's

00:17:15.319 --> 00:17:18.980
happening to that patient, to
make a prediction about a very

00:17:18.980 --> 00:17:21.740
late outcome.

00:17:21.740 --> 00:17:23.990
And unfortunately, as
Jonathan already pointed out,

00:17:23.990 --> 00:17:25.670
this data lives in
different silos.

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:28.640
To connect that data
is not a trivial task.

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:30.230
And this is another
step where AI

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:33.350
can help us to find for
a given set of patients

00:17:33.350 --> 00:17:38.360
the corresponding data for
an outcome in question.

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:40.520
And one thing we hope
to come out of this

00:17:40.520 --> 00:17:43.400
is that, instead of running on
dozens or hundreds of patients

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:46.340
per clinical study, we
can actually scale this up

00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:49.990
to thousands of
patients and beyond.

00:17:49.990 --> 00:17:53.960
Overall, this is going from
patient-structured data

00:17:53.960 --> 00:17:55.930
really to outcome-structured
data, which

00:17:55.930 --> 00:17:58.430
is just the way how data is not
organized in hospitals right

00:17:58.430 --> 00:17:59.520
now.

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:02.130
But now, this is where it starts
getting really interesting

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:04.730
because, once we have
this patient cohort

00:18:04.730 --> 00:18:06.847
and this overall overview here--

00:18:06.847 --> 00:18:09.180
what we're doing is we're
sending this data to the cloud

00:18:09.180 --> 00:18:11.804
component of our platform, which
we're building on Google Cloud

00:18:11.804 --> 00:18:12.900
Platform.

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:16.710
And here, we run an automated,
large-scale biomarker discovery

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:19.380
process to find
evidence for signals

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:24.090
in that data that correlates
with the outcome in question.

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:26.115
The goal at this
time is not to find

00:18:26.115 --> 00:18:30.540
a highly accurate classifier
just yet, but really

00:18:30.540 --> 00:18:35.250
to provide statistical
validation of that evidence

00:18:35.250 --> 00:18:37.940
for such a signal.

00:18:37.940 --> 00:18:40.230
And this discovery
process is entirely

00:18:40.230 --> 00:18:42.140
driven by the clinician.

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:46.470
There is no AI expertise
involved at that time.

00:18:46.470 --> 00:18:49.320
And this allows the
clinician to undergo

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:50.890
this as an iterative process.

00:18:50.890 --> 00:18:52.830
For example, you
might try at first

00:18:52.830 --> 00:18:57.120
to predict an outcome based
on the low-dose CT that

00:18:57.120 --> 00:18:59.467
was very early taken,
but it might turn out

00:18:59.467 --> 00:19:01.050
that in the low-dose
CT you just don't

00:19:01.050 --> 00:19:03.150
have this kind of information
that you're looking for.

00:19:03.150 --> 00:19:05.399
And this way, you can very
easily do another iteration

00:19:05.399 --> 00:19:07.680
where you don't bring in
CTs that were taken later

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:11.340
in the continuum of care.

00:19:11.340 --> 00:19:14.760
Once we have sufficiently
satisfied that we

00:19:14.760 --> 00:19:17.130
have such evidence
in our data, this

00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:19.950
is where the collaborative
aspect of our platform

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:21.460
can kick in.

00:19:21.460 --> 00:19:24.990
Namely, we can collaborate
with other clinicians

00:19:24.990 --> 00:19:27.780
from different hospitals
to enlarge the patient base

00:19:27.780 --> 00:19:33.390
to make sure more subpopulations
are covered more thoroughly.

00:19:33.390 --> 00:19:36.300
And we can collaborate with
AI researchers, AI experts,

00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:40.830
to start looking into building
a highly-optimized classifier

00:19:40.830 --> 00:19:45.210
for that biomarker, detector
for the biomarker in question.

00:19:45.210 --> 00:19:47.190
And once this has
been accomplished--

00:19:47.190 --> 00:19:49.710
and this is a part that is a
little bit beyond the scope

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:51.426
of this talk--

00:19:51.426 --> 00:19:55.950
productization process can be
undergone to really produce

00:19:55.950 --> 00:20:00.000
a clinically-created
biomarker in the end that

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:05.500
can truly affect patient care.

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:07.600
So if you allow me to
go a little bit more

00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:12.090
into detail how this
discovery process looks like.

00:20:12.090 --> 00:20:13.660
So as Jonathan
already said, it's

00:20:13.660 --> 00:20:18.460
based on recent advancements in
a field called radiomics, where

00:20:18.460 --> 00:20:20.380
the idea is to use
image features which

00:20:20.380 --> 00:20:22.900
are hidden to the human
eye to directly deduce

00:20:22.900 --> 00:20:24.520
a patient outcome.

00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:29.200
To do that, we bring the imaging
data together with the outcome

00:20:29.200 --> 00:20:32.240
information into Google Cloud.

00:20:32.240 --> 00:20:37.600
Here as an example, we have an
image of a presegmented liver,

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:39.610
but this really is
just a representation

00:20:39.610 --> 00:20:43.510
of a bigger patient data set.

00:20:43.510 --> 00:20:47.640
And afterwards, we
apply a suite of

00:20:47.640 --> 00:20:52.790
pre-trained, independent
feature extractors to that data.

00:20:56.370 --> 00:20:58.350
Possible examples
here for these feature

00:20:58.350 --> 00:21:00.933
extractors, just to give you an
idea, as those could be models

00:21:00.933 --> 00:21:03.720
that look for a blob
with a fussy context

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:09.180
or a blob into which a
lot of lines lead into.

00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:10.680
But all of these
feature extractors,

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:13.560
they are not outcome specific.

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:15.390
They can be used for
any kind of outcome.

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:17.520
And there, we have
thousands of them

00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:20.070
that we all apply--
every feature extractor

00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:22.710
to every single item
in the input data.

00:21:22.710 --> 00:21:24.980
And each feature extractor
will present us afterwards

00:21:24.980 --> 00:21:28.410
with an individual result.
We have a colorful map here

00:21:28.410 --> 00:21:30.286
to showcase a little bit better.

00:21:30.286 --> 00:21:32.160
But in the end, those
are just numeric values

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:34.200
on a continuous scale.

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:37.860
All of these results
are then aggregated,

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:40.710
and this allows us to
find the set of features

00:21:40.710 --> 00:21:45.870
which best correlates with
the outcome in question.

00:21:45.870 --> 00:21:49.440
And this set of features would
be our preliminary biomarker.

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:51.360
And just to reiterate,
the goal is really here

00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:55.320
to look for the
evidence of such signals

00:21:55.320 --> 00:21:58.440
corresponding to the features,
not necessarily the best

00:21:58.440 --> 00:22:01.110
possible accuracy at that time.

00:22:01.110 --> 00:22:05.790
Just to give it a little bit
of a technical twist, how

00:22:05.790 --> 00:22:10.470
we implemented that
on Google Cloud,

00:22:10.470 --> 00:22:12.910
we upload the data into
Google Cloud Storage.

00:22:12.910 --> 00:22:15.570
We do run a frontend
in the front

00:22:15.570 --> 00:22:19.830
based on Kubernetes engine,
which then sends all this data

00:22:19.830 --> 00:22:23.460
into this multitude of either
sort of TensorFlow models

00:22:23.460 --> 00:22:27.847
or extractors of other kinds for
which we could use Kubernetes

00:22:27.847 --> 00:22:28.930
Engine or Cloud Functions.

00:22:28.930 --> 00:22:32.610
The idea here really is to
make use of the scalability

00:22:32.610 --> 00:22:33.690
that we get out of this.

00:22:33.690 --> 00:22:38.850
So have on-demand scalability to
run this amount of computation.

00:22:38.850 --> 00:22:41.430
And for the results,
then we utilize

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:44.760
BitQuery to run our analysis
and present the result back

00:22:44.760 --> 00:22:48.330
to the clinician.

00:22:48.330 --> 00:22:52.050
Now, when we're talking about
feature extractors and models,

00:22:52.050 --> 00:22:54.330
et cetera in the
clinical context,

00:22:54.330 --> 00:22:56.940
there are a few peculiarities
about medical data

00:22:56.940 --> 00:22:58.980
that one really needs
to be aware about.

00:22:58.980 --> 00:23:00.520
It is different data.

00:23:00.520 --> 00:23:03.840
First of all, we have
highly imbalanced data sets.

00:23:03.840 --> 00:23:06.675
That means we're looking for
a needle in the haystack.

00:23:06.675 --> 00:23:08.960
So Jonathan mentions the
example of screening,

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:10.710
where we're looking
for a few patients who

00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:14.850
are affected by a disease in a
population of very, very many.

00:23:14.850 --> 00:23:18.290
And this gets even
worse for rare diseases.

00:23:18.290 --> 00:23:20.180
Also, what we're
trying to predict

00:23:20.180 --> 00:23:23.360
is potential treatment
efficacy for people

00:23:23.360 --> 00:23:25.830
for whom the standard
treatment would not work.

00:23:25.830 --> 00:23:28.970
So those are rather
the rare cases.

00:23:28.970 --> 00:23:32.940
Also, we are dealing with very
tiny lesions in large data.

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:35.760
So for example, say examples
of pulmonary embolism,

00:23:35.760 --> 00:23:37.730
there's one there, one there.

00:23:37.730 --> 00:23:40.090
Honestly, this is
a few gray pics

00:23:40.090 --> 00:23:42.650
in a bunch of a lot
more gray pixels.

00:23:42.650 --> 00:23:44.960
That's not an easy one to find.

00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:47.390
And to make matters
worse, this data

00:23:47.390 --> 00:23:49.750
tends to be not two, but
three- or four-dimensional

00:23:49.750 --> 00:23:51.410
for longitudinal studies.

00:23:51.410 --> 00:23:54.530
So a lot of the standard machine
learning pre-processing steps,

00:23:54.530 --> 00:23:56.270
such as scaling down
your data, it just

00:23:56.270 --> 00:24:00.690
won't work because you pretty
much lose all the information.

00:24:00.690 --> 00:24:04.350
To make matters worse, we have a
lot of variability in our data.

00:24:04.350 --> 00:24:05.910
Certain features,
certain contexts

00:24:05.910 --> 00:24:08.220
can manifest itself
differently depending

00:24:08.220 --> 00:24:14.180
on age, ethnicity, gender,
genotype, but also anamnesis

00:24:14.180 --> 00:24:15.060
of a patient.

00:24:15.060 --> 00:24:16.860
For example, we are
talking about trying

00:24:16.860 --> 00:24:19.780
to find the non-smokers
in a screening process.

00:24:19.780 --> 00:24:22.710
But if we train our model
primarily with smokers,

00:24:22.710 --> 00:24:27.360
most of them might exhibit
certain secondary symptoms

00:24:27.360 --> 00:24:28.536
such as emphysema.

00:24:28.536 --> 00:24:30.660
So we have to make sure
that our model doesn't just

00:24:30.660 --> 00:24:32.790
pick up on the emphysema,
but really goes

00:24:32.790 --> 00:24:34.840
for what we are looking for.

00:24:34.840 --> 00:24:37.500
And there are a lot of
device-specific artifacts

00:24:37.500 --> 00:24:38.670
in these images.

00:24:38.670 --> 00:24:41.610
So what we see here, this is not
how an imaging modality really

00:24:41.610 --> 00:24:42.520
acquires an image.

00:24:42.520 --> 00:24:46.140
In the case of an MRI, we
are dealing with disturbances

00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:47.500
in the magnetic field.

00:24:47.500 --> 00:24:49.860
This is just the
visual representation

00:24:49.860 --> 00:24:51.371
for human consumption.

00:24:51.371 --> 00:24:53.370
The process of going from
the real physical data

00:24:53.370 --> 00:24:57.690
to the visual data, this
is manufacturer-specific,

00:24:57.690 --> 00:25:00.450
and those are artifacts
that we need to account for.

00:25:00.450 --> 00:25:02.280
Luckily, we are not
left without tools.

00:25:02.280 --> 00:25:06.330
So we very often deploy a
mechanism called curriculum

00:25:06.330 --> 00:25:09.030
learning, for example,
to deal with tiny lesions

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:10.242
or imbalanced data.

00:25:10.242 --> 00:25:11.700
The idea behind
curriculum learning

00:25:11.700 --> 00:25:14.430
is that you really
start with a model that

00:25:14.430 --> 00:25:16.930
trains on a very small subset,
on a patch of the data.

00:25:16.930 --> 00:25:19.400
And then, as the
model learns this,

00:25:19.400 --> 00:25:22.380
we continuously
increase the amount

00:25:22.380 --> 00:25:24.960
of data, the amount of context
that be present to the model.

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:27.930
It works just as well
for imbalanced data sets.

00:25:27.930 --> 00:25:29.850
You can start with a
rather balanced data set

00:25:29.850 --> 00:25:33.720
and then go to
imbalanced bit by bit.

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:36.570
To account for the variability
and render specific artifacts,

00:25:36.570 --> 00:25:38.520
we just have to have
enough data to have

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:42.570
sufficient representation of all
these problems in our data set.

00:25:42.570 --> 00:25:45.210
But what I'm trying to
get it is that we're

00:25:45.210 --> 00:25:47.790
dealing with a lot of data,
and we're dealing with models

00:25:47.790 --> 00:25:52.740
that are deep, that are complex,
that need a long time to learn.

00:25:52.740 --> 00:25:55.170
And this was one of
the reasons for us

00:25:55.170 --> 00:25:59.212
to go onto Google Cloud with
this problem because here,

00:25:59.212 --> 00:26:01.170
of course, we can make
use of the near infinite

00:26:01.170 --> 00:26:02.830
amount of resources.

00:26:02.830 --> 00:26:06.450
And especially for us,
TensorFlow, ML Engine, TPUs,

00:26:06.450 --> 00:26:08.970
this whole stack
works really well

00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:11.340
to address the
complexity of our models

00:26:11.340 --> 00:26:14.280
because we get both the data
parallelism and the model

00:26:14.280 --> 00:26:16.920
parallelism with
reasonably low effort.

00:26:20.664 --> 00:26:25.120
Now, when I was talking about
feature extractors which

00:26:25.120 --> 00:26:28.570
are used by clinicians for
this discovery process,

00:26:28.570 --> 00:26:30.850
I mentioned that they are
pre-trained models, which

00:26:30.850 --> 00:26:35.230
of course raises the question,
how are these models trained?

00:26:35.230 --> 00:26:36.550
How are they built?

00:26:36.550 --> 00:26:39.430
How can AI experts
collaborate with clinicians

00:26:39.430 --> 00:26:42.280
to build such
feature extractors?

00:26:42.280 --> 00:26:45.970
And this is one of the use cases
for our proprietary platform,

00:26:45.970 --> 00:26:48.700
which we call Evidens.

00:26:48.700 --> 00:26:51.220
A bit of additional information.

00:26:51.220 --> 00:26:54.070
When working with
clinical data, as I'm sure

00:26:54.070 --> 00:26:57.410
all of you who work
in health care know,

00:26:57.410 --> 00:26:59.860
working with clinical
data is highly regulated.

00:26:59.860 --> 00:27:04.420
Some of the regulatory
frameworks are HIPAA, now GDPR.

00:27:04.420 --> 00:27:06.459
Health Canada has their own.

00:27:06.459 --> 00:27:08.500
And I don't want to give
an exhaustive overview--

00:27:08.500 --> 00:27:10.700
I can not give legal advice--

00:27:10.700 --> 00:27:14.270
but what it boils down
to for us is this.

00:27:14.270 --> 00:27:16.900
First of all, we of course
have to anonymize the data.

00:27:16.900 --> 00:27:18.700
HIPAA, for example,
provides a list

00:27:18.700 --> 00:27:21.520
of fields that needs to
be removed before data can

00:27:21.520 --> 00:27:24.310
leave the hospital's premises.

00:27:24.310 --> 00:27:27.820
And we need to apply
deanonymization,

00:27:27.820 --> 00:27:29.920
deidentification
of the data, which

00:27:29.920 --> 00:27:32.020
means assigning
random identifiers

00:27:32.020 --> 00:27:35.950
rather than using the original
hospital-generated ones.

00:27:35.950 --> 00:27:38.350
But it does it does
not stop there.

00:27:38.350 --> 00:27:40.660
Data for clinical
research can only

00:27:40.660 --> 00:27:46.160
be used based on patient consent
and institutional approval.

00:27:46.160 --> 00:27:47.680
There are IRB ERBs.

00:27:47.680 --> 00:27:50.290
That stands for Institutional
or Ethics Review Board

00:27:50.290 --> 00:27:53.320
depending on where one lives.

00:27:53.320 --> 00:27:57.390
And they grant ethics
approval for clinical studies.

00:27:57.390 --> 00:27:59.580
The problem with this
consent and those approvals

00:27:59.580 --> 00:28:02.026
is that they can be revoked.

00:28:02.026 --> 00:28:03.650
So a patient could
walk into a hospital

00:28:03.650 --> 00:28:06.070
and say, yeah, I
have past consent

00:28:06.070 --> 00:28:09.430
for this technical study,
but I changed my mind.

00:28:09.430 --> 00:28:11.300
And in the case of
such a revocation,

00:28:11.300 --> 00:28:15.020
we have to be able to
delete the patient's data,

00:28:15.020 --> 00:28:19.240
but also, to be on the safe
side, any kind of derived data.

00:28:19.240 --> 00:28:22.480
This can be something simple,
such as I had a CT scan

00:28:22.480 --> 00:28:24.760
and I cropped it
and saved this crop.

00:28:24.760 --> 00:28:26.990
But this could also be
something more complicated,

00:28:26.990 --> 00:28:28.510
especially in the
context of machine

00:28:28.510 --> 00:28:32.920
learning where models are
represented by weights.

00:28:32.920 --> 00:28:35.530
Weights are affected by
the patient's data that

00:28:35.530 --> 00:28:37.300
went into the training process.

00:28:37.300 --> 00:28:40.660
Now, if we are thinking about
models such as autoencoders

00:28:40.660 --> 00:28:45.460
and generative models, it's hard
to guarantee that there is not

00:28:45.460 --> 00:28:47.530
some detailed information
about that patient

00:28:47.530 --> 00:28:50.170
inside the model that
would allow to regenerate

00:28:50.170 --> 00:28:51.160
the patient's data.

00:28:51.160 --> 00:28:53.380
So to be on the
safe side, we said

00:28:53.380 --> 00:28:56.080
we even want to delete those
weights in the case of such

00:28:56.080 --> 00:28:57.620
a revocation.

00:28:57.620 --> 00:29:00.010
Of course, being
able to revoke means

00:29:00.010 --> 00:29:03.030
we have to actually trace all
of this in the first place.

00:29:03.030 --> 00:29:06.490
We have to make sure we know
for every single patient what

00:29:06.490 --> 00:29:10.030
happened to his or
her data in the cloud.

00:29:10.030 --> 00:29:11.800
And this does not
just mean the data

00:29:11.800 --> 00:29:15.670
once it gets uploaded, but
also any subsequent processing

00:29:15.670 --> 00:29:18.850
step that we might
apply to the data.

00:29:18.850 --> 00:29:24.260
And to give a fairly high-level
overview of how we achieve this

00:29:24.260 --> 00:29:24.760
in GCP--

00:29:29.210 --> 00:29:33.230
there we go-- we upload data
from hospitals into the cloud.

00:29:33.230 --> 00:29:35.780
We run a data ingestion
process, which brings that data

00:29:35.780 --> 00:29:37.700
into a date lake.

00:29:37.700 --> 00:29:40.430
But before any kind of
operation is happening

00:29:40.430 --> 00:29:42.470
on a piece of data,
we always make

00:29:42.470 --> 00:29:48.080
sure we create a trace log
entry that records for which

00:29:48.080 --> 00:29:53.180
patient what data has
been done what to.

00:29:53.180 --> 00:29:58.260
Any kind of data processing that
we run in the cloud we first

00:29:58.260 --> 00:30:02.790
create a dedicated data set for,
which means we create a Google

00:30:02.790 --> 00:30:05.645
Cloud Storage bucket into which
we copy data out of our data

00:30:05.645 --> 00:30:07.800
lake to make sure
no single process

00:30:07.800 --> 00:30:09.150
can ever access this data.

00:30:09.150 --> 00:30:11.490
This is really a
highly-secure data storage

00:30:11.490 --> 00:30:13.630
that nobody has access to.

00:30:13.630 --> 00:30:15.840
For every process, we
create a dedicated data set,

00:30:15.840 --> 00:30:18.870
and this is something for
us very easy to trace.

00:30:18.870 --> 00:30:22.770
And it's just easy to show
that this works reliably

00:30:22.770 --> 00:30:24.840
because only what goes
into this data set

00:30:24.840 --> 00:30:27.120
can be accessed by the
data processing step.

00:30:27.120 --> 00:30:29.460
Data processing, this
could be image processing,

00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:32.150
model training,
anything like this.

00:30:32.150 --> 00:30:34.790
Any kind of output produced
by a data processing

00:30:34.790 --> 00:30:38.210
step, such as crop
images or model weights,

00:30:38.210 --> 00:30:40.280
go in a Google
Cloud Storage bucket

00:30:40.280 --> 00:30:44.600
which has been specifically
created for that operation--

00:30:44.600 --> 00:30:48.680
which, again in itself, is link
back to all of the input data

00:30:48.680 --> 00:30:50.670
and sufficiently traced.

00:30:50.670 --> 00:30:54.620
So we have really a
traceability chain

00:30:54.620 --> 00:30:58.280
from every bit of patient data
throughout our entire system

00:30:58.280 --> 00:31:00.550
to any kind of
result that we store.

00:31:04.126 --> 00:31:05.750
If you allow me to
go a little bit more

00:31:05.750 --> 00:31:08.810
into detail on the first
piece of this puzzle, the data

00:31:08.810 --> 00:31:13.990
ingestion, the first
step happens on premise

00:31:13.990 --> 00:31:16.630
where we receive
a cohort and then

00:31:16.630 --> 00:31:19.420
we upload it into our inbox,
which is hospital specific.

00:31:19.420 --> 00:31:22.790
We can make use of
a service account

00:31:22.790 --> 00:31:25.960
such is specific
to the hospital.

00:31:25.960 --> 00:31:28.360
So only this hospital
has access to this inbox.

00:31:28.360 --> 00:31:31.900
We have an ingestion process
which then copies the data

00:31:31.900 --> 00:31:34.780
into our data lake
only after making sure

00:31:34.780 --> 00:31:38.700
that the proper trace log
entries are being created.

00:31:38.700 --> 00:31:41.280
One thing that we
also did to the inbox

00:31:41.280 --> 00:31:43.590
is we created all
the deletion rules

00:31:43.590 --> 00:31:47.610
because, when a patient
revocation request comes in,

00:31:47.610 --> 00:31:49.290
you have a certain
amount of time

00:31:49.290 --> 00:31:51.610
to comply with that request.

00:31:51.610 --> 00:31:54.670
And we really tried to build and
robustness from the beginning.

00:31:54.670 --> 00:31:56.370
So what if this
ingestor doesn't run

00:31:56.370 --> 00:32:00.480
and the data just sits in the
inbox bucket as an orphan?

00:32:00.480 --> 00:32:03.750
If a patient revocation
request came in,

00:32:03.750 --> 00:32:05.610
this data would still exist.

00:32:05.610 --> 00:32:06.810
We would have a problem.

00:32:06.810 --> 00:32:09.270
So we just delete this
data after a couple of days

00:32:09.270 --> 00:32:12.140
automatically.

00:32:12.140 --> 00:32:15.500
But now, to the real
meat of the matter.

00:32:15.500 --> 00:32:19.040
How can our AI researchers
use our platform

00:32:19.040 --> 00:32:20.720
for their everyday work?

00:32:20.720 --> 00:32:23.480
And our goal here was to
give maximum flexibility

00:32:23.480 --> 00:32:25.640
to AI researchers.

00:32:25.640 --> 00:32:29.240
And often, that involves running
not just single trainings,

00:32:29.240 --> 00:32:33.430
but long workflows
with multiple stages

00:32:33.430 --> 00:32:36.770
and conditional steps of
pre-processing, training,

00:32:36.770 --> 00:32:39.550
analysis, visualization,
et cetera.

00:32:39.550 --> 00:32:41.900
So we want to enable
researchers to do this while

00:32:41.900 --> 00:32:43.880
still maintaining full
control over the data

00:32:43.880 --> 00:32:47.060
and ensuring traceability.

00:32:47.060 --> 00:32:50.690
And the overall idea for us was
we don't have the mountain come

00:32:50.690 --> 00:32:53.300
to the prophet, but the
prophet to the mountain, which

00:32:53.300 --> 00:33:00.220
means we have a secured area
where all the data storage

00:33:00.220 --> 00:33:02.440
and execution is happening.

00:33:02.440 --> 00:33:04.960
And in any kind of processing
step via the training data,

00:33:04.960 --> 00:33:08.380
free processing is encapsulated
as a container or zip

00:33:08.380 --> 00:33:11.050
file containing ML
Engine code and then

00:33:11.050 --> 00:33:14.680
submitted along with a
query into our system where

00:33:14.680 --> 00:33:16.990
we run this query
on the data service

00:33:16.990 --> 00:33:21.099
and produce a data packet,
which I mentioned before,

00:33:21.099 --> 00:33:22.390
which is given to the training.

00:33:22.390 --> 00:33:25.792
The training runs on that data
and produces all the output

00:33:25.792 --> 00:33:27.250
in an output packet,
which can then

00:33:27.250 --> 00:33:30.340
be reingested for subsequent
use by later stages

00:33:30.340 --> 00:33:32.417
in that workflow.

00:33:32.417 --> 00:33:34.000
And again, one of
the important things

00:33:34.000 --> 00:33:37.210
is that each one of these
steps, each one of these arrows

00:33:37.210 --> 00:33:41.290
leads to according entries in
our trace log-- which means,

00:33:41.290 --> 00:33:44.320
even for this device data that
gets reingested into the data,

00:33:44.320 --> 00:33:48.830
we always know which original
patient that came from.

00:33:48.830 --> 00:33:51.340
A few other Google
techniques that we use

00:33:51.340 --> 00:33:54.740
is we have a per
training or per process

00:33:54.740 --> 00:33:58.490
service accounts, which are
the only ones then allowed

00:33:58.490 --> 00:33:59.445
to access this bucket.

00:33:59.445 --> 00:34:01.820
This way, we can really make
sure that every training can

00:34:01.820 --> 00:34:03.686
only access the data
it's supposed to,

00:34:03.686 --> 00:34:05.810
but at the same time that
every bit of data we have

00:34:05.810 --> 00:34:07.601
can only be accessed
by the training that's

00:34:07.601 --> 00:34:11.520
supposed to access the data.

00:34:11.520 --> 00:34:15.810
And by linking every
data along this way

00:34:15.810 --> 00:34:18.449
back to the original
individual patient

00:34:18.449 --> 00:34:21.840
data and the patient
identifier, we

00:34:21.840 --> 00:34:26.470
can maintain that
requirement of traceability.

00:34:26.470 --> 00:34:28.221
Starting to go a
little bit faster

00:34:28.221 --> 00:34:30.179
because I'm noticing
we're running a little bit

00:34:30.179 --> 00:34:32.220
short on time.

00:34:32.220 --> 00:34:34.889
We the on top of this, to
make it really easy to use,

00:34:34.889 --> 00:34:37.350
created a lot of
CLIs and frontends

00:34:37.350 --> 00:34:39.630
and function as a service
and Cloud Functions

00:34:39.630 --> 00:34:42.179
to model of these workflows.

00:34:42.179 --> 00:34:44.100
And one example I
wanted to give is

00:34:44.100 --> 00:34:47.100
we have one project which
involves colonoscopy,

00:34:47.100 --> 00:34:50.550
where we receive colonoscopy
videos from which

00:34:50.550 --> 00:34:51.719
we have to extract frames.

00:34:51.719 --> 00:34:53.139
These frames have
to be prepared.

00:34:53.139 --> 00:34:54.960
We have to identify
the type of lighting

00:34:54.960 --> 00:34:59.070
that was used, for which we
have our own models to do this.

00:34:59.070 --> 00:35:03.150
Then there is some manual
tagging involved by clinicians.

00:35:03.150 --> 00:35:05.190
And only after
all of this can we

00:35:05.190 --> 00:35:08.010
actually start building
cohorts because only then

00:35:08.010 --> 00:35:09.580
do we know what
we're dealing with.

00:35:09.580 --> 00:35:11.880
So then, we can build
cohorts and then

00:35:11.880 --> 00:35:13.030
feed this into training.

00:35:13.030 --> 00:35:16.350
And each one of these steps
undergoes this full loop.

00:35:16.350 --> 00:35:19.277
So that final model that
comes out of this training,

00:35:19.277 --> 00:35:21.360
we can still trace back
to every single video that

00:35:21.360 --> 00:35:25.940
might have affected that model.

00:35:25.940 --> 00:35:27.620
And of course, the
reason why we're

00:35:27.620 --> 00:35:32.540
going through all this hassle
is to ensure revocability.

00:35:32.540 --> 00:35:34.400
And as I said before,
a patient might

00:35:34.400 --> 00:35:38.390
walk any day into the hospital
and say, I gave your consent,

00:35:38.390 --> 00:35:40.130
but I changed my mind.

00:35:40.130 --> 00:35:41.850
I want you to undo this.

00:35:41.850 --> 00:35:45.860
It has to happen on premise
because we have to look up

00:35:45.860 --> 00:35:47.570
the deidentified identifier.

00:35:47.570 --> 00:35:49.505
But the important
piece is really

00:35:49.505 --> 00:35:52.340
we sent this request
into the cloud

00:35:52.340 --> 00:35:56.870
where we run this process that
we call nomen est omen revoker.

00:35:56.870 --> 00:35:59.210
And this process can then
use all this information

00:35:59.210 --> 00:36:01.470
that we previously
stored in the trace log

00:36:01.470 --> 00:36:05.240
to identify every single
location of related data

00:36:05.240 --> 00:36:07.730
to that patient in
be it our data lake

00:36:07.730 --> 00:36:12.710
indices, any kind of bucket
running trainings, metadata.

00:36:12.710 --> 00:36:14.300
Anything can be deleted--

00:36:14.300 --> 00:36:16.250
will be deleted, sorry--

00:36:16.250 --> 00:36:18.830
and a report will be
generated, which is then

00:36:18.830 --> 00:36:20.000
sent back to the hospital.

00:36:20.000 --> 00:36:21.530
And that's just one
of the requirements

00:36:21.530 --> 00:36:23.488
that we have to have this
full loop from the on

00:36:23.488 --> 00:36:26.290
premise through the cloud
back to the hospital.

00:36:26.290 --> 00:36:30.980
Now, bridging back to the
biomarker discovery process,

00:36:30.980 --> 00:36:32.660
of course all these
feature extractors

00:36:32.660 --> 00:36:36.560
that we mentioned before are
models that have been trained

00:36:36.560 --> 00:36:38.060
through this platform.

00:36:38.060 --> 00:36:40.360
Those are models that are
deployed, that run somewhere,

00:36:40.360 --> 00:36:44.010
that are used for the
biomarker discovery process.

00:36:44.010 --> 00:36:46.050
In the case of
such a revocation,

00:36:46.050 --> 00:36:49.010
what this allows us to do
is to automatically retrain

00:36:49.010 --> 00:36:51.890
the affected models
just in this case

00:36:51.890 --> 00:36:53.780
without the data
of that one patient

00:36:53.780 --> 00:36:57.710
that just issued a
revocation request.

00:36:57.710 --> 00:36:59.690
We can retrain
these models, making

00:36:59.690 --> 00:37:02.990
sure all accuracy requirements
for every possible

00:37:02.990 --> 00:37:05.390
subpopulation was
still being met,

00:37:05.390 --> 00:37:08.660
and then automatically redeploy
this model, and this way

00:37:08.660 --> 00:37:13.320
of ensuring a continuous service
for our biomarker discovery

00:37:13.320 --> 00:37:15.690
platform.

00:37:15.690 --> 00:37:20.480
So this is where we are today.

00:37:20.480 --> 00:37:23.090
Ongoing work that we're
currently pursuing

00:37:23.090 --> 00:37:26.660
is that we really want to
improve the trustability

00:37:26.660 --> 00:37:27.590
of our platform.

00:37:27.590 --> 00:37:30.080
We want to make it
easy for patients

00:37:30.080 --> 00:37:33.650
to trust the platform
that we're building

00:37:33.650 --> 00:37:37.700
to make people comfortable
when they contribute

00:37:37.700 --> 00:37:40.035
to clinical studies.

00:37:40.035 --> 00:37:43.590
Of course, one obvious thing
no talk in this time and age

00:37:43.590 --> 00:37:46.310
can lift without
mentioning blockchain

00:37:46.310 --> 00:37:47.940
is to use blockchain.

00:37:47.940 --> 00:37:50.340
And one of the areas where
we think it could really

00:37:50.340 --> 00:37:54.090
be beneficial is to use
blockchain's capabilities

00:37:54.090 --> 00:37:58.560
as an immutable ledger,
where we can issue receipts

00:37:58.560 --> 00:38:01.680
to patients and hospitals saying
yes, we've received this data

00:38:01.680 --> 00:38:04.400
or we've received the
revocation request.

00:38:04.400 --> 00:38:08.010
And there would be
no way to wriggle out

00:38:08.010 --> 00:38:09.600
of that responsibility
that comes

00:38:09.600 --> 00:38:13.830
with this data or that
revocation request.

00:38:13.830 --> 00:38:16.110
In the end, is it the
solution to all our problems?

00:38:16.110 --> 00:38:16.730
Probably not.

00:38:16.730 --> 00:38:18.605
And if anybody in the
audience has any ideas,

00:38:18.605 --> 00:38:21.690
I would love to talk
about that after the talk.

00:38:21.690 --> 00:38:24.467
I don't know how to model
deletion with blockchain.

00:38:24.467 --> 00:38:26.550
How do I guarantee that
something has been deleted

00:38:26.550 --> 00:38:28.350
and there's no copy
anywhere left of it?

00:38:28.350 --> 00:38:31.770
So there's always a component
of trust left in such a system.

00:38:31.770 --> 00:38:34.290
You have to trust a system or
you have to trust a company

00:38:34.290 --> 00:38:36.480
or you have to trust
a regulator or you

00:38:36.480 --> 00:38:39.350
have to trust a
blockchain implementation.

00:38:39.350 --> 00:38:40.770
A fully trustless system?

00:38:40.770 --> 00:38:43.800
No, but we can really
try to improve trust

00:38:43.800 --> 00:38:45.710
for patients and hospitals.

00:38:45.710 --> 00:38:47.370
We are also looking
into implementing

00:38:47.370 --> 00:38:49.360
certain cryptographic
guarantees, proxy

00:38:49.360 --> 00:38:55.590
via encryption, to be able to
say we can use cryptography

00:38:55.590 --> 00:38:58.110
to guarantee that only
certain processes can access

00:38:58.110 --> 00:39:00.510
the data that they are
supposed to access.

00:39:00.510 --> 00:39:02.400
And one next step
of that could be

00:39:02.400 --> 00:39:05.070
this could allow us to
be really further patient

00:39:05.070 --> 00:39:10.090
engagement by modeling
that process of consent

00:39:10.090 --> 00:39:12.850
through cryptographic
guarantees, where a patient--

00:39:12.850 --> 00:39:17.650
by re-encrypting keys specific
to a certain clinical study

00:39:17.650 --> 00:39:20.360
or training to be done--

00:39:20.360 --> 00:39:23.680
with their own key could
explicitly give their consent

00:39:23.680 --> 00:39:26.077
to that clinical study.

00:39:26.077 --> 00:39:28.660
We've been talking a lot about
data and in the context of data

00:39:28.660 --> 00:39:32.260
mainly about patient data,
but there's more to it.

00:39:32.260 --> 00:39:36.070
All of these consents
and authorizations,

00:39:36.070 --> 00:39:38.140
they have data associated
with themselves.

00:39:38.140 --> 00:39:43.450
Those are forms, processes,
and we would like to model this

00:39:43.450 --> 00:39:44.950
as well to really
be able to give

00:39:44.950 --> 00:39:49.450
a holistic end-to-end of
the different processes that

00:39:49.450 --> 00:39:53.890
are involved on clinical sites
with respect traceability,

00:39:53.890 --> 00:39:57.910
data in the cloud,
and revocability.

00:39:57.910 --> 00:40:01.780
And we believe that there
are certain areas where

00:40:01.780 --> 00:40:03.790
the centralized
learning in the cloud

00:40:03.790 --> 00:40:07.450
might not be as suitable
or even impossible.

00:40:07.450 --> 00:40:12.640
For example, if you want to run
a machine learning operation

00:40:12.640 --> 00:40:16.870
on data that requires
information that otherwise gets

00:40:16.870 --> 00:40:18.990
removed through the
anonymization process,

00:40:18.990 --> 00:40:22.030
it's almost not possible
to run this in the cloud.

00:40:22.030 --> 00:40:24.910
So for these few
cases, we are looking

00:40:24.910 --> 00:40:29.410
into federated learning, where
data could stay on premise

00:40:29.410 --> 00:40:31.900
and we only keep the
control in the cloud.

00:40:31.900 --> 00:40:35.140
But of course, this is a
less desirable approach just

00:40:35.140 --> 00:40:38.670
to do the hardware maintenance
and all of these headaches.

00:40:42.820 --> 00:40:44.892
Back to Jonathan.

00:40:44.892 --> 00:40:47.350
JONATHAN KANEVSKY: So I'm pretty
sure if Dr. Larry Reed was

00:40:47.350 --> 00:40:50.210
to time travel from
the '60s, his head

00:40:50.210 --> 00:40:53.180
would spin with the
complexity of how important it

00:40:53.180 --> 00:40:56.340
is to think about some of the
things we're thinking about.

00:40:56.340 --> 00:40:58.490
But ultimately,
to summarize, what

00:40:58.490 --> 00:41:01.640
we've wanted to share with
you is our approach to create

00:41:01.640 --> 00:41:04.170
this collaboration ecosystem--

00:41:04.170 --> 00:41:06.050
which is powered by
the Google Cloud--

00:41:06.050 --> 00:41:08.510
where we bring together
health experts,

00:41:08.510 --> 00:41:13.100
engineering experts in a
way where they can not only

00:41:13.100 --> 00:41:15.560
use AI without
the health experts

00:41:15.560 --> 00:41:17.510
can use AI without
the knowledge of AI,

00:41:17.510 --> 00:41:20.090
but to do so on a
large impactful scale

00:41:20.090 --> 00:41:21.399
across a large network.

00:41:21.399 --> 00:41:22.940
So thank you very
much for your time,

00:41:22.940 --> 00:41:25.280
and we'd be happy to
take any questions.

00:41:25.280 --> 00:41:28.630
[MUSIC PLAYING]

